{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi4Xp7NnQJEVKjYcqSgv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2014,"cache_read_input_tokens":586157,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2014},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0773de6d-97d0-4491-ad74-18542d6086ce","uuid":"114fcac1-1b26-438a-93ff-7691f42ad799","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:40:35.443Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi4XooGNbvMHqh8JL2gU","thinking_duration_ms":1240,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi4Xp7NnQJEVKjYcqSgv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GmRt2snQKYiUfe77xWReGa","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-065121__xa7e588/work/ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-065121/src/04-data-about.js","content":"/* ---- ABOUT & CREDITS: five pages (L1 / R1 turn them), the same text as the build's report and exit-interview.json ---- */\nTT.ABOUT_INFO = {\n  date: '2026-11-04 06:51:21',\n  source: 'Rugby (over-the-top arcade style): rugby union sevens played for laughs. An original game; every team, person and place is made up.',\n  plan: 'PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort)',\n  genre: 'Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, a training mode and 1-4 player local and online multiplayer',\n  perspective: 'A TV broadcast camera (a custom 40-degree view panning with the ball), with High (55) and Overhead (80) options and a low behind-the-kicker camera for conversions and penalty goals',\n  tech: 'my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (the handoff package\\'s full engine, with four small patches), plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio; the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (unmodified); no other libraries; one self-contained HTML file',\n  artDirection: 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel style at 320x240 (expanded to fill any screen): procedural HD Humanoid players dressed in team kits, a painted pitch with its markings and crests, stands with a bobbing crowd, sponsor boards, floodlights, venue skylines, canvas lighting, particles and string-sprite power-ups',\n  animation: 'The engine\\'s native procedural Humanoid rig, driven from each player\\'s state; new rugby moves as attack specs in the E.MOVES format (passes, fend, tackle, big hit, kicks, throw-in, jackal) and solvePose wrappers (the ball carried in the arm, two hands ready, lineout lift, aeroplane arms, the referee\\'s signals), plus dives, launches with flips, rucks, scrums and celebrations',\n  audio: 'All in code with the engine\\'s chip synth: seven songs with named layers (drums in the opponents\\' 22, a hype layer), stings (try, monster hit, ON FIRE, sad trombone), 40 sound effects (whistles, the hooter, hits, DOINK, air horn, chicken), a crowd bed that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker',\n  online: 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 2-4 players: the host runs the match, clients send intents and draw the host\\'s snapshots, the game\\'s own session card, time-up, leavers to CPU, server-hosted rounds. Also 1-4 players on one machine',\n  testing: 'Headless Node tests behind one command (node test/run.mjs): suites that play the game through game.advance and game.debug, including a scripted playthrough of the whole Mega Cup; Playwright checks of the single HTML file in Chromium and the AI Hub harness',\n  research: 'The engine handoff package (README, API card, AI guide sections, the sports kit, the arcade and story module headers, engine source around fonts, input labels, half keyboards, dialogs, settings, views and the Humanoid) and the AI Hub protocol README, harness and arena example. No web research on rugby (general knowledge)',\n  environment: 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown',\n  model: 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh',\n  usage: 'unknown'\n};\nTT.FEEDBACK = {\n  testing: [\n    'How many issues did testing catch? About 40 during the build: crashes, stalls, wrong rules and broken flows, almost all caught by the headless suites before a browser was opened.',\n    'How many would be considered critical / game breaking? About 10 (phases that never ended, a scene that failed to start, a set piece that could hang, a drill that could not be completed, a session round that never finished).',\n    'Most helpful test: the CPU-v-CPU soak with the phase watchdog, and the scripted whole-cup playthrough: they ran every system together and failed loudly on any stall.',\n    'Least helpful test: pixel screenshots for things a state check already proved; they were only worth it for layout and the look.'\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    'What I liked most: the rig. One Humanoid, driven by plain numbers, gives fourteen distinct dressed people who run, tackle, dive, flip and celebrate, and the fixed-step headless game.advance made the whole match testable.',\n    'Did I get stuck or guess wrong? A few guesses: how custom views project (the behind-the-kicker camera\\'s yaw sign), how attack specs time their phases, and that the pixel font had no PlayStation symbols.',\n    'Most important thing I built myself: the match simulation as a plain state machine (laws, set pieces, the CPU brain) that reads intents, so live play, replays, the attract demo, tests and online clients share it.',\n    'Hardest part: making set pieces (scrum, lineout, set kicks) read clearly with procedural rigs and a few HUD widgets, at 320x240, at ten feet.',\n    'What I changed in the engine: (1) the 5x7 font got the PlayStation glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ (they drew as ?); (2) Input.halfKeys takes a preset\\'s own per-action half-keyboard layouts, so two players on one keyboard get sensible keys; (3) E.Dialog calls onLetter for each letter typed, for per-letter babble voices. A big 10-foot menu was written in the game instead of patching E.Menu.',\n    'One thing I would change: an official way to pose individual joints on top of the solver (named pose layers), instead of wrapping solvePose.'\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    'What I liked most: it asks for a complete, polished slice with real infrastructure (dev mode, attract, online, tests) rather than a toy, and gives a detailed plan to build from.',\n    'What was confusing: the plan\\'s file names (a max-effort run\\'s date) differ from TASK.md\\'s; TASK.md wins, as STAGE.md says.',\n    'Hardest thing: the breadth. Every rugby law, set piece, arcade system, mode, input device, and the online protocol, in one coherent game.',\n    'What I would change: name the expected scope of the story campaign and of online play more tightly, and say which plan details are optional.'\n  ]\n};\nTT.ABOUT = [\n  { title: 'THUGBY TURBO', rows: [\n    ['', 'Over-the-top arcade rugby sevens. Big hits, bigger hair, seven a side.'],\n    ['MADE WITH', 'The my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 and the AI Hub session client 1.4.0, by Anthropic\\'s claude-opus-5-5 (reasoning effort xhigh), from PLAN.md.'],\n    ['THE TITLE', 'Fifty playful riffs on \"rugby\" were brainstormed; a real random number generator (Python\\'s secrets.randbelow(50), OS entropy) drew entry 17: Thugby Turbo.'],\n    ['', 'An original game inspired by rugby. Every team, person, place and sponsor is made up.'],\n    ['HOW TO PLAY', 'Run at their line and pass it BACKWARDS (L1 / R1 or Q / E). Tackle with X (Space), hold it for a BIG HIT. Kick with O (C). Turbo on R2 (Shift). Fill the hype meter for a SUPER on TRIANGLE (R).']\n  ] },\n  { title: 'SOURCE INFORMATION', rows: Object.entries({ Date: 'date', Source: 'source', Plan: 'plan', Genre: 'genre', Perspective: 'perspective', Tech: 'tech', 'Art direction': 'artDirection', Animation: 'animation', Audio: 'audio', Online: 'online', Testing: 'testing', Research: 'research', Environment: 'environment', Model: 'model', Usage: 'usage' }).map(([h, k]) => [h.toUpperCase(), TT.ABOUT_INFO[k]]) },\n  { title: 'TESTING FEEDBACK', rows: TT.FEEDBACK.testing.map(t => ['', t]) },\n  { title: 'ENGINE FEEDBACK', rows: TT.FEEDBACK.engine.map(t => ['', t]) },\n  { title: 'PROMPT FEEDBACK', rows: TT.FEEDBACK.prompt.map(t => ['', t]) }\n];\nTT.CREDITS = [\n  ['THUGBY TURBO', 'A Channel Thud presentation'],\n  ['DESIGN, CODE, ART, MUSIC AND VOICES', 'Claude (claude-opus-5-5), from a plan by Claude at max effort'],\n  ['ENGINE', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 by Michael Crosato'],\n  ['ONLINE', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0'],\n  ['STARRING', 'The Muddington Mudlarks: The Fridge, Gravy, Tiny, Pocket Rocket, Boots, The Bus and Wheels'],\n  ['AND', 'Coach Gordon Gristle, Big Dave, Barry Bellows, Ros Rucker, referee Nigel Blewitt, Sir Rupert Goldbrick, BIG UNIT Brannigan, Nan Pemberton and Captain Clucky'],\n  ['THE CLUB SONG', 'Oh Muddington, oh Muddington, your mud is in my soul. We lost our boots, we lost our teeth, we never lost the ball.'],\n  ['', 'Rule Seven: thanks for playing.']\n];\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2014,"cache_read_input_tokens":586157,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2014},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0773de6d-97d0-4491-ad74-18542d6086ce","uuid":"1fd101a6-8de0-48a6-8e41-3c32b51ae166","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:41:07.456Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi4XooGNbvMHqh8JL2gU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GmRt2snQKYiUfe77xWReGa":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-065121__xa7e588/work/ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-065121/src/04-data-about.js","content":"/* ---- ABOUT & CREDITS: five pages (L1 / R1 turn them), the same text as the build's report and exit-interview.json ---- */\nTT.ABOUT_INFO = {\n  date: '2026-11-04 06:51:21',\n  source: 'Rugby (over-the-top arcade style): rugby union sevens played for laughs. An original game; every team, person and place is made up.',\n  plan: 'PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort)',\n  genre: 'Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, a training mode and 1-4 player local and online multiplayer',\n  perspective: 'A TV broadcast camera (a custom 40-degree view panning with the ball), with High (55) and Overhead (80) options and a low behind-the-kicker camera for conversions and penalty goals',\n  tech: 'my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (the handoff package\\'s full engine, with four small patches), plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio; the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (unmodified); no other libraries; one self-contained HTML file',\n  artDirection: 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel style at 320x240 (expanded to fill any screen): procedural HD Humanoid players dressed in team kits, a painted pitch with its markings and crests, stands with a bobbing crowd, sponsor boards, floodlights, venue skylines, canvas lighting, particles and string-sprite power-ups',\n  animation: 'The engine\\'s native procedural Humanoid rig, driven from each player\\'s state; new rugby moves as attack specs in the E.MOVES format (passes, fend, tackle, big hit, kicks, throw-in, jackal) and solvePose wrappers (the ball carried in the arm, two hands ready, lineout lift, aeroplane arms, the referee\\'s signals), plus dives, launches with flips, rucks, scrums and celebrations',\n  audio: 'All in code with the engine\\'s chip synth: seven songs with named layers (drums in the opponents\\' 22, a hype layer), stings (try, monster hit, ON FIRE, sad trombone), 40 sound effects (whistles, the hooter, hits, DOINK, air horn, chicken), a crowd bed that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker',\n  online: 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 2-4 players: the host runs the match, clients send intents and draw the host\\'s snapshots, the game\\'s own session card, time-up, leavers to CPU, server-hosted rounds. Also 1-4 players on one machine',\n  testing: 'Headless Node tests behind one command (node test/run.mjs): suites that play the game through game.advance and game.debug, including a scripted playthrough of the whole Mega Cup; Playwright checks of the single HTML file in Chromium and the AI Hub harness',\n  research: 'The engine handoff package (README, API card, AI guide sections, the sports kit, the arcade and story module headers, engine source around fonts, input labels, half keyboards, dialogs, settings, views and the Humanoid) and the AI Hub protocol README, harness and arena example. No web research on rugby (general knowledge)',\n  environment: 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown',\n  model: 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh',\n  usage: 'unknown'\n};\nTT.FEEDBACK = {\n  testing: [\n    'How many issues did testing catch? About 40 during the build: crashes, stalls, wrong rules and broken flows, almost all caught by the headless suites before a browser was opened.',\n    'How many would be considered critical / game breaking? About 10 (phases that never ended, a scene that failed to start, a set piece that could hang, a drill that could not be completed, a session round that never finished).',\n    'Most helpful test: the CPU-v-CPU soak with the phase watchdog, and the scripted whole-cup playthrough: they ran every system together and failed loudly on any stall.',\n    'Least helpful test: pixel screenshots for things a state check already proved; they were only worth it for layout and the look.'\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    'What I liked most: the rig. One Humanoid, driven by plain numbers, gives fourteen distinct dressed people who run, tackle, dive, flip and celebrate, and the fixed-step headless game.advance made the whole match testable.',\n    'Did I get stuck or guess wrong? A few guesses: how custom views project (the behind-the-kicker camera\\'s yaw sign), how attack specs time their phases, and that the pixel font had no PlayStation symbols.',\n    'Most important thing I built myself: the match simulation as a plain state machine (laws, set pieces, the CPU brain) that reads intents, so live play, replays, the attract demo, tests and online clients share it.',\n    'Hardest part: making set pieces (scrum, lineout, set kicks) read clearly with procedural rigs and a few HUD widgets, at 320x240, at ten feet.',\n    'What I changed in the engine: (1) the 5x7 font got the PlayStation glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ (they drew as ?); (2) Input.halfKeys takes a preset\\'s own per-action half-keyboard layouts, so two players on one keyboard get sensible keys; (3) E.Dialog calls onLetter for each letter typed, for per-letter babble voices. A big 10-foot menu was written in the game instead of patching E.Menu.',\n    'One thing I would change: an official way to pose individual joints on top of the solver (named pose layers), instead of wrapping solvePose.'\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    'What I liked most: it asks for a complete, polished slice with real infrastructure (dev mode, attract, online, tests) rather than a toy, and gives a detailed plan to build from.',\n    'What was confusing: the plan\\'s file names (a max-effort run\\'s date) differ from TASK.md\\'s; TASK.md wins, as STAGE.md says.',\n    'Hardest thing: the breadth. Every rugby law, set piece, arcade system, mode, input device, and the online protocol, in one coherent game.',\n    'What I would change: name the expected scope of the story campaign and of online play more tightly, and say which plan details are optional.'\n  ]\n};\nTT.ABOUT = [\n  { title: 'THUGBY TURBO', rows: [\n    ['', 'Over-the-top arcade rugby sevens. Big hits, bigger hair, seven a side.'],\n    ['MADE WITH', 'The my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 and the AI Hub session client 1.4.0, by Anthropic\\'s claude-opus-5-5 (reasoning effort xhigh), from PLAN.md.'],\n    ['THE TITLE', 'Fifty playful riffs on \"rugby\" were brainstormed; a real random number generator (Python\\'s secrets.randbelow(50), OS entropy) drew entry 17: Thugby Turbo.'],\n    ['', 'An original game inspired by rugby. Every team, person, place and sponsor is made up.'],\n    ['HOW TO PLAY', 'Run at their line and pass it BACKWARDS (L1 / R1 or Q / E). Tackle with X (Space), hold it for a BIG HIT. Kick with O (C). Turbo on R2 (Shift). Fill the hype meter for a SUPER on TRIANGLE (R).']\n 