{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi8Le2JrQaQj6z9otHqd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **THUGBY TURBO**, an over-the-top arcade rugby sevens game, as one self-contained file: `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-065121.html`. It sits in the folder root next to `exit-interview.json`, which parses with every field answered. `series` is empty, which the spec allows (\"or empty\"). All dev files are in the `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-065121/` subfolder.\n\n`node test/run.mjs --browser --hub` passed 67 of 67 checks. That covers 64 headless checks and two Chromium checks of the HTML file at 1920×1080, 1280×720 and an upright phone. It also covers the real AI Hub harness in all four of its configurations, plus the game running on its own. I looked at screenshots for the visuals, but nobody has played it by hand with a real controller.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Match:** passing only goes backwards (L1/R1 or Q/E), with dummies, offloads and interceptions. Tackles lead to rucks; there are scrums, lineouts, kick-offs, 22 drop-outs, conversions and penalty goals taken from a behind-the-kicker camera, and the penalty choice. Knock-ons, forward passes, advantage, cards and an actual sin bin are called by a referee who only sees what's in front of him.\n- **Arcade layer:** turbo, big and monster hits, sidesteps, spins and fends. A hype meter unlocks two supers, a player who scores twice running goes ON FIRE, and the crowd throws on five power-ups. Each of the four stadiums has a hazard (mud, a chicken mascot, flame jets, lightning). The referee can be flattened, and big moments get instant replays.\n- **Modes:** the Mega Cup story (prologue, four matches, victory, a closing pub scene, credits, with CONTINUE after a loss), Exhibition for 1–4 local players, a Training Ground of nine drills each with a SHOW ME demo, and Records.\n- **Other requirements:**\n  - **Dev mode:** reachable from the pause menu, with 36 live sliders and a reset to defaults, plus cheats, every forced event, debug overlays and jumps to any part of the game.\n  - **Attract mode:** a demo starts after 10 seconds idle on the title or menus and walks through every system; any input exits.\n  - **Controls:** PlayStation glyphs by default, with an Xbox option in Settings; native keyboard and mouse; prompts follow the last device used.\n  - **Touch-only devices:** show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button.\n  - **Audio:** all in code, including babble voices.\n  - **Online:** AI Hub shared-world play for 2–4 players.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:51:21\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style): rugby union sevens played for laughs. Every team, person and place is made up.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where its file names differed from TASK.md, I used TASK.md's.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, a training mode and 1–4 player local and online play. The camera is a TV broadcast view at 40° that pans with the ball, with High (55°) and Overhead options and a low camera behind the kicker for set kicks.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with four small patches, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio, and the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 unmodified. No other libraries; one HTML file.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's PS1-era pixel style at 320×240, expanded to fill the screen. Kit-dressed 3D-rig players, a painted pitch with crests, stands with a crowd, sponsor boards, floodlights, venue skylines, lighting, particles and sprite power-ups.\n- **Animation:** the engine's native Humanoid rig driven from each player's state. New rugby moves are written as engine attack moves (passes, fend, tackle, big hit, kicks, throw-in, jackal) and pose overrides (ball carry, lineout lift, aeroplane arms, the referee's signals), plus dives, flips, rucks, scrums and celebrations.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth only: seven songs with layers that come in during play, stings, about 40 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Protocol v1 in shared-world mode for 2–4 players: the host runs the match, the other copies send their moves and draw what the host sends. It shows its own lobby card and handles time-up, leavers and server-hosted rounds. Also 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** one command, `node test/run.mjs`, runs 64 headless checks that play the game through the engine's test hooks, including a scripted playthrough of the whole Mega Cup. `--browser` adds the Chromium checks and `--hub` runs the real AI Hub harness. Screenshots only for the look.\n- **Research:** the engine's handoff package (README, API card, guide sections, the sports kit, module headers, parts of the engine source) and the AI Hub README, harness and arena example. No web research on rugby.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown; session time about 1 hour 45 minutes.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 38. The headless suites caught 26 (crashes, stalls, wrong rules, moves that couldn't be triggered, drills that couldn't be finished, balance). The AI Hub harness in a real browser caught 2, and screenshots caught about 10 layout problems.\n- **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 7:\n  - The game failed to boot twice: once headless, and once only in a real browser.\n  - Kicks never fired for human players.\n  - Kicks and passes launched from the wrong spot.\n  - The online lobby card could swallow the Ready press.\n  - Three drills couldn't be completed.\n- **Most helpful test:** the drills and the whole-cup playthrough, which play the game the way a player does. They found the dead kick button and the double-tap spin that could never trigger. The AI Hub harness came second, because it found a crash only a real browser shows.\n- **Least helpful test:** the defensive-shape metric. I set its thresholds myself and they moved with every balance change.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the Humanoid rig, where a few plain numbers give fourteen distinct dressed players. The fixed-step headless mode made whole matches testable in milliseconds.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** The yaw sign for a custom camera view, that the engine's storage keeps nothing when the browser has no localStorage, and that the pixel font had no PlayStation symbols.\n- **Most important thing I built:** the match simulation as a plain state machine that only reads player inputs. Live play, replays, the attract demo, drills, tests and online clients all share it.\n- **Hardest part:** making scrums, lineouts, rucks and set kicks read clearly with procedural rigs at 320×240, from across a room.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** four patches, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`:\n  - PlayStation glyphs (plus two symbols) added to the pixel font.\n  - Proper layouts for two players sharing one keyboard.\n  - A per-letter hook on dialogs, for the babble voices.\n  - A width and position option on dialogs, so commentary fits between the player cards.\n- **One thing I'd change:** let games blend named poses (ball carry, arms up, a referee signal) on top of the rig's own posing, instead of overriding the posing code by hand.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** it asks for a complete, polished slice with real infrastructure rather than a toy, and the plan made the scope concrete.\n- **Confusing:** the plan's file names and date belong to a different run. STAGE.md settles it: TASK.md wins.\n- **Hardest:** the breadth. Every law and set piece, the arcade layer, five modes, three input devices and an online protocol in one game.\n- **Would change:** say which plan details are optional when time is short, and give a target length for the story and the online round.\n\nThe same four sections are in the game under ABOUT & CREDITS."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":782,"cache_read_input_tokens":828658,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":782},"output_tokens":787,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0773de6d-97d0-4491-ad74-18542d6086ce","uuid":"ff427794-749a-48ef-9e93-1b62177b0981","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:30:54.523Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi8LdaH5X4frGm1RemAw"}
