{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi96QnJW11LKnQLnyEpt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I went through each point the stop hook raised. Two were real gaps, and one turned up a real bug, now fixed. Everything is rebuilt, and `node test/run.mjs --browser --hub` now passes 71 of 71 checks: 64 headless, 6 in the browser, and the AI Hub harness.\n\n**What the hook raised, and what I found:**\n- **Online harness:** it was already running all four configurations, but it only printed failures, so the transcript showed nothing. It now prints every check. All four pass every one: two players (14 checks), four players at 150 ms ± 50 with 5% loss (14), host leaves (14), and server-hosted (15). The game also runs normally outside a session.\n- **Xbox option in Settings, in a browser:** a new browser test clicks the real \"Controller buttons\" row, which sits in Settings › Display. It goes from PlayStation (the default, even with an Xbox pad plugged in) to \"Xbox A B X Y\". With a pad as the last device used, the in-match prompts then read A, X, B, Y, LB and RB, and the screenshot confirms it.\n- **Controller:** I still haven't played it on a real controller. The browser test fakes a standard-mapping DualSense through `navigator.getGamepads`, the same API a real pad uses:\n  - Options at the title opens the menu, the d-pad moves the focus, ✕ picks and ○ goes back.\n  - In a match, the left stick runs the player and R1 passes.\n  - Prompts show ✕, □, ○, △, L1, R1 and R2, which the screenshot confirms.\n- **Bug found and fixed:** on touch-only phones, the engine quietly switched the touch stick back on each time a scene started, so a finger could still steer. It's now permanently off. The browser test confirms that, and that the SETTINGS button opens Settings and the attract demo still runs on a phone.\n- **Mouse, tested in the browser:**\n  - Hovering a menu item moves the focus and a click picks it.\n  - Hovering a teammate highlights him, and a click passes to him.\n  - Holding and releasing the right button kicks toward the cursor.\n- **Attract and dev mode, tested in the browser:** after 10 idle seconds the demo starts, and a key returns to the title. At 1920×1080 and 1280×720 the pause menu's DEV MODE opens the panel.\n- **Slider count:** 36 is right. The panel's \"1/37\" counts the RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS row plus the 36 sliders, and a direct count of the panel's rows confirms it.\n- **Balance:** I tuned it myself against CPU-vs-CPU measurements, not human play. With the Mudlarks on Pro, they beat the Badgers 16-0-0, are roughly even with the Gulls (8 wins, 5 losses, 3 draws), trail the Ironsides (4-10-2), and lose to Megacorp in the final more often than not (4-10-2).\n\n`ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-065121.html` is in the folder root, along with `exit-interview.json`, which parses with every field answered (`series` is empty, which the spec allows). The four sections below are also in the game under ABOUT & CREDITS.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:51:21\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style): rugby union sevens played for laughs. Every team, person and place is made up.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where its file names differed from TASK.md, I used TASK.md's.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, a training mode and 1–4 player local and online play. The camera is a TV broadcast view at 40° that pans with the ball, with High and Overhead options and a low camera behind the kicker for set kicks.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with four small patches, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio, and the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 unmodified. No other libraries; one HTML file.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's PS1-era pixel style at 320×240, expanded to fill the screen. Kit-dressed 3D-rig players, a painted pitch with crests, stands with a crowd, sponsor boards, floodlights, venue skylines, lighting, particles and sprite power-ups.\n- **Animation:** the engine's native Humanoid rig driven from each player's state. New rugby moves are written as engine attack moves (passes, fend, tackle, big hit, kicks, throw-in, jackal) and pose overrides (ball carry, lineout lift, aeroplane arms, the referee's signals), plus dives, flips, rucks, scrums and celebrations.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth only: seven songs with layers that come in during play, stings, about 40 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Protocol v1 in shared-world mode for 2–4 players: the host runs the match, the other copies send their moves and draw what the host sends. It shows its own lobby card and handles time-up, leavers and server-hosted rounds. Also 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** `node test/run.mjs` runs 64 headless checks that play the game through the engine's test hooks, including a scripted playthrough of the whole Mega Cup. `--browser` adds 6 Chromium checks: a simulated standard-mapping controller with PlayStation, then Xbox, prompts; keyboard and mouse; the attract mode; a touch-only phone; TV and laptop sizes with DEV MODE. `--hub` runs the real AI Hub harness in all four configurations. Screenshots only for the look. No real controller was available; the pad was simulated.\n- **Research:** the engine's handoff package (README, API card, guide sections, the sports kit, module headers, parts of the engine source) and the AI Hub README, harness and arena example. No web research on rugby.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown; session time about 1 hour 45 minutes for the build, plus this verification round.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 39. The headless suites caught 26 (crashes, stalls, wrong rules, moves that couldn't be triggered, drills that couldn't be finished, balance). A real browser caught 3: two by the AI Hub harness, and the touch stick switching itself back on for touch-only phones. Screenshots caught about 10 layout problems.\n- **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 7:\n  - The game failed to boot twice: once headless, and once only in a real browser.\n  - Kicks never fired for human players.\n  - Kicks and passes launched from the wrong spot.\n  - The online lobby card could swallow the Ready press.\n  - Three drills couldn't be completed.\n- **Most helpful test:** the drills and the whole-cup playthrough, which play the game the way a player does. They found the dead kick button and the double-tap spin that could never trigger. The AI Hub harness came second, because it found a crash only a real browser shows.\n- **Least helpful test:** the defensive-shape metric. I set its thresholds myself and they moved with every balance change.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the Humanoid rig, where a few plain numbers give fourteen distinct dressed players. The fixed-step headless mode made whole matches testable in milliseconds.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** The yaw sign for a custom camera view, that the engine's storage keeps nothing when the browser has no localStorage, that the pixel font had no PlayStation symbols, and that the engine's touch setting re-enables the touch stick on every scene.\n- **Most important thing I built:** the match simulation as a plain state machine that only reads player inputs. Live play, replays, the attract demo, drills, tests and online clients all share it.\n- **Hardest part:** making scrums, lineouts, rucks and set kicks read clearly with procedural rigs at 320×240, from across a room.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** four patches, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`:\n  - PlayStation glyphs (plus two symbols) added to the pixel font.\n  - Proper layouts for two players sharing one keyboard.\n  - A per-letter hook on dialogs, for the babble voices.\n  - A width and position option on dialogs, so commentary fits between the player cards.\n- **One thing I'd change:** let games blend named poses (ball carry, arms up, a referee signal) on top of the rig's own posing, instead of overriding the posing code by hand.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** it asks for a complete, polished slice with real infrastructure rather than a toy, and the plan made the scope concrete.\n- **Confusing:** the plan's file names and date belong to a different run. STAGE.md settles it: TASK.md wins.\n- **Hardest:** the breadth. Every law and set piece, the arcade layer, five modes, three input devices and an online protocol in one game.\n- **Would change:** say which plan details are optional when time is short, and give a target length for the story and the online round."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1397,"cache_read_input_tokens":862526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1397},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0773de6d-97d0-4491-ad74-18542d6086ce","uuid":"bcd59d64-9646-4c4e-a830-faff014e1866","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:40:54.086Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi96QEp35Jt25vjcPhFS"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML and exit-interview.json exist and the four sections appear in the final reply, but the transcript does not establish that every TASK.md feature is implemented without stubs. Several are not verified: the shared-world AI Hub mode and its 2-4 player configurations passed the harness, but no human controller was ever used and the controller path is only simulated; the TASK.md 'Performance' and dev-mode sliders are a count mismatch the assistant explained (36 sliders vs the '1/37' panel row); and the story campaign's endings, the Exhibition setup of the weather and time choices, and several in-game scenes (for example the About pages' Testing/Engine text which was rewritten later) were not tested end to end. The assistant also states its own balance tuning was done against CPU-only play, so the 'playable and balanced' requirement rests on self-assessment."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0773de6d-97d0-4491-ad74-18542d6086ce","uuid":"1e265d47-ff96-48d5-89e8-0bb39cc90864","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:41:01.202Z","isSynthetic":true}
